<h2>Engineering decisions</h2>

<p><strong>Fail softly at system boundaries.</strong> A buyer’s request is stored before email or ERPNext work begins. If a downstream service is unavailable, the enquiry remains intact and can be reconciled later.</p>

<p><strong>Validate at the boundary.</strong> Zod schemas normalize incoming data before it reaches admin views, portal records or ERP integration. Every later feature works against the same known shape.</p>

<p><strong>Separate public and operational surfaces.</strong> Public pages can remain fast and cacheable while authenticated tools run on demand. The split keeps the marketing experience responsive without weakening the internal product.</p>

<CaseStudyMedia image={products} alt="Reve product catalogue with garment categories and an integrated cost calculator" caption="04 / Product discovery and costing before a buyer enters the request flow." />

<h2>Result</h2>

<p>The platform now carries production traffic, structures quote and sample requests, gives staff a working operations surface and connects buyer intake to ERPNext. It remains active infrastructure rather than a finished brochure: workflows, dependencies and admin needs continue to change.</p>

<CaseStudyMedia image={homeMobile} alt="Reve Stitching homepage displayed on a mobile viewport" caption="05 / The public experience on mobile, where many buyers first arrive." />

<h2>Lesson</h2>

<blockquote><p>A business website becomes a product when the information it collects starts driving the operation behind it.</p></blockquote>

